Q: autodoc and svelte 5 A: <p>Autodocs with Svelte 5 and SvelteKit is still rough — Storybook's autodoc parser doesn't fully support Svelte 5's runes syntax yet.</p>
<p>For <code>$props()</code>, the workaround is to define your props as a TypeScript interface and reference it explicitly in your story's <code>meta</code>:</p>
<pre><code>// MyComponent.stories.ts
import type { Meta } from '@storybook/sveltekit';
import MyComponent from './MyComponent.svelte';

const meta: Meta&lt;typeof MyComponent&gt; = {
  component: MyComponent,
  tags: ['autodocs'],
};

export default meta;
</code></pre>
<p>For the component description, the JSDoc block needs to be directly above the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag in Svelte 5:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!-- MyComponent.svelte --&gt;
&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;
  /** This is my component description */
  interface Props {
    /** Description for this prop */
    name: string;
  }
  let { name }: Props = $props();
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Storybook's Svelte plugin reads JSDoc from the <code>Props</code> interface, not from HTML comments. The <code>&lt;!-- @component --&gt;</code> syntax was Svelte 4 — it's not picked up by autodocs in Svelte 5.</p>
